steps.reasoning_content:
The index.html doesn't have an `active` class on its nav links because it's the home page and none of the main nav items (Products, Community, Markets, Brokers) point to `index.html`. This is actually fine — on the home page, no nav item should be highlighted as active. But wait, I should check that the `initNavActive` JS function handles this correctly.

Looking at the JS:
```javascript
function initNavActive() {
  const page = window.location.pathname.split('/').pop() || 'index.html';
  document.querySelectorAll('.nav__links a').forEach(link => {
    const href = link.getAttribute('href');
    if (href === page || (page === '' && href === 'index.html') || (page === 'index.html' && href === 'index.html')) {
      link.classList.add('active');
    }
  });
}
```

Since none of the nav links point to `index.html`, no link will be highlighted as active on the home page, which is correct behavior.

Everything looks good. Let me also verify the overall structure by checking the `<!DOCTYPE html>` and closing `</html>` tags on all files.
